[Windows charmap Encoding Error] (noted by user): 2026-09-08

Incident: "Warning: Could not generate full analysis report ('charmap' codec can't encode character '✓' in position 16: character maps to <undefined>)"

Context:
User attempted to run summarize.py with analysis report generation on Windows. The script failed when trying to write output containing a Unicode checkmark character (✓, U+2713).

Error:
The checkmark character is not representable in Windows's default 'charmap' encoding (CP1252), which only supports ASCII and a limited set of extended characters. The character appears in reporting.py line 28 in the format consistency status output.

Root cause:
Unicode characters like ✓ (checkmark) and ✗ (cross/X) were used directly in console output without encoding consideration. Windows console defaults to charmap encoding which cannot represent these characters. This is a common issue when cross-platform code doesn't account for Windows encoding limitations.

Resolution:
1. Replaced ✓ with [OK] in reporting.py line 28
2. Replaced ✗ with [ERROR] in reporting.py line 37
3. Both replacements use ASCII-safe characters compatible with all platforms/encodings

Impact: Script now runs successfully on Windows without encoding errors. Output is still clear and readable with [OK]/[ERROR] markers.

Lesson: When writing console output in cross-platform Python code, avoid Unicode characters (especially symbols) that won't encode in Windows charmap. Use ASCII equivalents like [OK], [ERROR], or text descriptions instead.

