[Missing Import - NameError on Full Dataset] (noted by Claude): 2026-09-09

Incident: trial_loading.py raised NameError: name 're' is not defined when summarize.py was run against the full results dataset.

Context:
While verifying a TrialKey refactor (adding format_hardness as an identity field) end-to-end, ran `python summarize.py --no-prompt -v --analysis` with no experiment filter, exercising every experiment in results/. This immediately crashed inside _is_txt1_leading_number_exception, which calls re.match(...) -- but trial_loading.py never imports the re module.

Error:
NameError: name 're' is not defined, raised from trial_loading.py line 92 (re.match), propagating up through _track_item_level_issues -> _track_item_quality_issues -> update_aggregations_from_trial -> process_trial_set -> summarize_results, uncaught anywhere in that chain.

Root cause:
This exception path only fires for a .txt1 (numberedText) file whose item carries a "leading_punctuation" issue -- exactly the case the function exists to suppress as expected (numbered lines legitimately start with a digit). A prior verification pass for the 2026-09-08 TrialSet/TrialKey refactor used `--experiment animals_big` only, whose .txt1 files happened not to trigger this exact branch, so the missing import went unnoticed. Likely introduced when this function was written or moved into trial_loading.py without carrying its `re` dependency along.

Resolution:
Added `import re` to trial_loading.py's import block.

Impact: Full-dataset summarize.py runs (and any narrower run that happens to include a numberedText file with a flagged leading-punctuation item) now complete instead of crashing.

Lesson: A test run scoped to one experiment/filter is not a full verification -- it can miss code paths gated on data characteristics (file type + specific issue combination) that only appear elsewhere in the dataset. Verify refactors against the unfiltered dataset at least once before considering them confirmed.

