[Silent Wrong Default - formatHardness Dropped From Aggregated Metadata] (noted by Claude): 2026-09-09

Incident: generate_report.py's report.html labeled every trial's format hardness as "soft", including trials that were actually "hard".

Context:
While threading format_hardness through generate_report.py (part of the same TrialKey identity fix), passed format_hardness into get_cleanup_data_for_combo and verified against report.html. Every deepseekr18b plot-section -- a model whose result files are exclusively hard-hardness (-fh- in the filename) -- rendered data-hardness="soft" and an empty Cleanup tooltip, despite results.json and quality.json both correctly recording "hard" for those same files.

Error:
aggregate_items_by_format_and_model's per-combo metadata dict (result[key]["metadata"]) was built with only experiment/format/prompt/model/temperature -- formatHardness was never copied in from the source entry's metadata. Every downstream reader (_build_combo_info, _generate_combo_figures, _build_plot_section_html) then read metadata.get("formatHardness", "soft"), which always hit the default.

Root cause:
This metadata dict was written as an explicit field-by-field literal rather than copying the fields needed from entry['metadata']; formatHardness was added to result files' metadata (parse_filename_metadata) after this dict literal was written, and never backfilled here. A pre-existing bug, not introduced by the current session's edits, but silent and highly visible once looked for: every plot title and cleanup tooltip in every previously-generated report.html has shown "soft" as the format hardness regardless of the trial's actual hardness.

Resolution:
Added `format_hardness = metadata.get('formatHardness', 'unknown')` and included it as `"formatHardness": format_hardness` in the stored per-combo metadata dict.

Impact: report.html now correctly labels hard-hardness combos and their Cleanup tooltips show real quality data instead of silently reading an always-empty "soft" bucket of quality.json.

Lesson: A dict built as an explicit field-by-field literal from a richer source dict is a recurring risk here -- adding a new field to the source (metadata) does not automatically appear in a literal copy built before the field existed. When a new metadata field is added, grep for existing metadata-to-metadata copy literals that might need to carry it too.

