[Silently Broken Feature - Case-Inconsistency Detection Never Fired] (noted by Claude): 2026-09-09

Incident: TrialSet.detect_case_inconsistencies() could never detect a real inconsistency, for any dataset, because the data it reads was already deleted by the time it ran.

Context:
While extending TrialKey with format_hardness and then experiment (two prior commits this session), touched case_values_agg twice -- adding a hardness nesting level, then an experiment nesting level -- without ever asking why a dict called "case values aggregation" existed alongside a completely separate TrialSet-based case-consistency check. The user asked "What else have you forgotten?" after two rounds of fixes, prompting a closer look at exactly this dict.

Error:
update_aggregations_from_trial (trial_loading.py) called trial.metadata.pop("case", "lower") during per-trial aggregation. process_trial_set runs this for every trial in every TrialSet BEFORE _flag_case_inconsistencies is ever called (summarize.py's summarize_results calls them in that order). Because TrialSet.trials holds the same Trial object references used elsewhere (not copies), every trial's "case" key was already gone from trial.metadata by the time TrialSet.detect_case_inconsistencies() -> extract_case_values() read trial.metadata.get("case", "lower") -- it always saw the default "lower", so it could never observe more than one distinct case value across a trial set.

Root cause:
Likely a leftover from an earlier design where case_values_agg (write-only, never read by anything) was meant to be the mechanism for cross-trial case-consistency checking, before that logic was rewritten to read trial.metadata directly via TrialSet -- without noticing the pop() a few lines above would now run before the new TrialSet-based check ever executed. Confirmed via production data before the fix: 0 of 9,351 results.json entries retained a "case" key, and zero non-empty inconsistent_case entries existed anywhere in quality.json, across the whole dataset.

Resolution:
Left "case"/"consistentCase" in trial.metadata during aggregation; moved the strip to _compute_quality_and_consistency, immediately after _flag_case_inconsistencies has read them. Also removed case_values_agg entirely (confirmed dead -- nothing reads it) and format_aggs[ext]['agg'] (six per-format Counter dicts, same write-only pattern), found while investigating this.

Impact: quality.json now contains real inconsistent_case findings (84 in the current dataset, e.g. gpt35turbo producing "lower" case in one trial and "mixed" in another trial of the same set) where it had zero before. report.html's Cleanup tooltip now surfaces "Inconsistent Case" for affected combos. results.json still correctly omits "case"/"consistentCase" from every entry (9,351 of 9,351) -- the strip is deferred, not skipped.

Lesson: A write-only aggregation dict sitting next to a working feature that reads its data from somewhere else entirely is a signal to ask why, not just carry it forward through the next refactor. Also: verify a "detection" feature by checking whether it *ever* fires on real data (grep the output for the flag it's supposed to set), not just that the code path runs without error -- an always-empty result and a broken result look identical from the outside.

